final_text:
Fix applied in `src/attr/_make.py:601` (`_make_cached_property_getattr`). The no-original-`__getattr__` branch of the generated code changed from:

```python
if hasattr(super(), '__getattr__'):
    return super().__getattr__(item)
original_error = f"'{self.__class__.__name__}' object has no attribute '{item}'"
raise AttributeError(original_error)
```

to:

```python
try:
    return super().__getattribute__(item)
except AttributeError:
    if not hasattr(super(), '__getattr__'):
        raise
    return super().__getattr__(item)
```

Now, when an attribute lookup lands in the generated `__getattr__` for something that isn't a cached property, normal attribute resolution is retried via `super().__getattribute__`, so an `AttributeError` raised from inside a property/cached-property body propagates unchanged instead of being replaced by "'A' object has no attribute 'f'". A base class `__getattr__` is still consulted when one exists, and the "object has no attribute" message for genuinely missing attributes is now the one `object.__getattribute__` produces.

No tests were edited. `python -m pytest -q -o pythonpath=src tests/test_slots.py` → 51 passed, 1 skipped (the pre-existing PyPy-only skip).
